00:04:01.516 --> 00:04:06.636
Falcon 9 first stage recovery is the engineering angle on a single Merlin performing what engineers

00:04:06.636 --> 00:04:07.776
call a hover slam.

00:04:08.436 --> 00:04:13.716
The engine loses the ability to modulate if it goes below roughly 40% throttle, so the

00:04:13.716 --> 00:04:18.616
The booster has to light the landing burn already at 40-70% while it is still going fast,

00:04:19.076 --> 00:04:23.696
and whatever impulses left has to bring speed to zero at the exact instant of touchdown.

00:04:24.456 --> 00:04:27.736
That single engine floor is why the profile has to be continuous.

00:04:28.256 --> 00:04:31.056
There is no lower setting to drop to in the middle of the burn.

00:04:31.676 --> 00:04:35.756
If the minimum throttle still pushes harder than the booster weighs, you cannot hover.

00:04:36.216 --> 00:04:41.456
You can only slam, matching leftover energy against leftover distance, so both hit zero together.

00:04:41.456 --> 00:04:46.756
weather. Super Heavy does not try to do that with one engine. It lights 13 Raptors to cancel

00:04:46.756 --> 00:04:51.836
most of the speed, then cuts to the three center engines for the final approach. Each

00:04:51.836 --> 00:04:57.216
of those three still modulates between roughly 40% and full thrust, which gives the Super Heavy

00:04:57.216 --> 00:05:02.816
booster a near hover the tower arms need for capture. Going from 13 engines down to three

00:05:02.816 --> 00:05:07.376
is on the order of a four-fold cut-in thrust, the course break, and then the remaining

00:05:07.376 --> 00:05:09.016
Trio handles the fine control.

00:05:09.616 --> 00:05:14.256
Too many engines at the end would keep even a 40% floor too strong for a hover.

00:05:14.876 --> 00:05:18.336
Three engines bring that floor into the band the arms can work with.

00:05:18.856 --> 00:05:23.796
Three engines can also be throttled a little unequally, so the super heavy booster can correct

00:05:23.796 --> 00:05:28.396
a small lean that a single merlin, thrusting only along its own axis, cannot.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:34.416
Falcon 9 therefore keeps landing legs, because a one engine hover slam leaves little margin

00:05:34.416 --> 00:05:39.156
for being in the wrong place, while Super Heavy deletes the legs and saves the mass that

00:05:39.156 --> 00:05:41.196
would otherwise fly to orbit every time.

00:05:41.816 --> 00:05:45.756
The catch hardware now has to do the last bit of positioning the legs used to do.

